The file system name includes the extension.

kCFURLNameKey deleted the path extension before taking the last component, so NSURLNameKey answered
Cider for Cider.md. macOS documents that key as the name the FILE SYSTEM provides, which includes
the extension; kCFURLLocalizedNameKey is the display name and is the one allowed to hide it.

Found while writing the resource value section of the probe for 0133, not from an application
symptom, and every application on the roster asks for resource values.

probe_bookmark_identity checks it with a control: a name that HAS no extension must come back
unchanged, so a fix that simply stopped trimming anything could not pass by accident.

--- a/NSURL.m
+++ b/NSURL.m
@@ -228,10 +228,10 @@
     // NOTE: not all of the properties are currently supported, however the most common ones should have quasi-reasonable implementations
     if (CFEqual(key, kCFURLNameKey))
     {
-        //Key for the resource’s name in the file system, returned as a CFString object.
-        CFURLRef trimmed = CFURLCreateCopyDeletingPathExtension(kCFAllocatorDefault, url);
-        value = CFURLCopyLastPathComponent(trimmed);
-        CFRelease(trimmed);
+        /* THE NAME IN THE FILE SYSTEM, which includes the extension. Deleting it here answered
+         * Cider for Cider.md. NSURLLocalizedNameKey below is the display name, and that is the one
+         * allowed to hide an extension. */
+        value = CFURLCopyLastPathComponent(url);
     }
     else if (CFEqual(key, kCFURLLocalizedNameKey))
     {
